In lstc, you can write inline asm, which is a special kind of asm, managed by the compiler, its platform independend
and has special syntax, you can also reference variables and so on in it, its not compiler managed, just to give you
freedom to do some exotic things, also it handles itself extending and so on, you just do what you need

syntax example:
let: int q = 567;
core::asm! {
add(&q, 7)
};
available ops

move
sub
add
syscall
mul
imul
and so on

asm -- collect
core::asm.collect collects variables into registers, and then passes to a syscall, its a short way to write long syscalls
syntax:
core::asm.collect(SYSCALL, args...)
it also works in raw asm blocks
